title: See it, then delete it
chapter: 22
type: lesson
skills: visual-char
par: 20
minutes: 4
vscode: click before the bracket, drag to the closing one, Delete
open: refunds.md

@brief
Chapter 1 let you look at visual mode. This chapter puts it to work,
and starts by being honest about where it belongs.

`v` starts a character-wise selection at the cursor. Every motion you
already know — `w`, `f`, `$`, `}`, a search — now extends the selection
instead of just moving. When the region is what you want, press an
operator. `d` deletes it, `c` changes it, `y` yanks it. The same letters
as always, just pressed *after* the region instead of before it.

The runbook has a parenthetical that runs over a line break and adds
nothing. Remove it, including the space before the opening bracket, so
the sentence reads:

    Refunds older than thirty days are refused by the payments service and
    the order is marked with a reason code instead.

@keys
| `v` | start a character-wise selection |
| `/pattern` | in visual mode, extends the selection to the match |
| `d` | delete the selection |
| `Esc` | drop the selection, change nothing |

@hints
Search your way to the space before `(this`. Start the selection there.
--
In visual mode a search is just another motion. `/)` extends the
selection to the closing bracket, and because visual selections are
inclusive, the bracket itself is in.
--
`/ (this` `Enter`, `v`, `/)` `Enter`, `d`. Then save.

@solution
    / (this<Enter> v /)<Enter> d :wq<Enter>

Seventeen keystrokes. `d/)` from normal mode would have stopped one
character short — the search motion is exclusive — and the fix for that
(`d/)/e`) is the kind of thing nobody remembers at 11pm. In visual mode
you watched the highlight reach the bracket and then pressed `d`. That
is the honest case for `v`: when the region is easier to see than to
name, select it, check it, act.

The rest of the chapter is about knowing when that is true and when it
is not. Most of the time an operator plus a motion or a text object says
the same thing in fewer keys, and lesson 11 measures the difference.

@start refunds.md
# Refund runbook

Refunds older than thirty days are refused by the payments service (this
was the behaviour of the legacy gateway too, before the migration) and
the order is marked with a reason code instead.

The reason code is `REFUND_WINDOW_EXPIRED` and finance can see it on
the order.

@expected refunds.md
